<docs-article
  title="Theming"
  description="marko-ui components are styled entirely with semantic CSS variables. Change the variables to change the look — you never have to touch component classes."
  href="/docs/theming"
  toc=TOC
>
  <p>
    Every component in the registry uses semantic Tailwind utilities like
    <code>bg-background</code>, <code>text-muted-foreground</code>, and
    <code>border-border</code>. Those map to CSS custom properties defined in
    <code>packages/registry/default/styles/globals.css</code>, which the registry ships to your
    project as its own <code>globals.css</code>.
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=USAGE/>

  <h2 id="convention">Token Convention</h2>

  <p>
    Tokens come in background/foreground pairs. The base token is the surface color; the
    <code>-foreground</code> token is the text and icon color that sits on that surface. The
    <code>-background</code> suffix is omitted from the surface token, so <code>primary</code>
    pairs with <code>primary-foreground</code>.
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=PAIR/>

  <p>
    Given those two variables, <code>bg-primary text-primary-foreground</code> is guaranteed to be
    a legible combination in both light and dark mode. Sticking to the pairs is what keeps a
    re-themed app readable without auditing every component.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-4">
    <for|swatch| of=SWATCHES by=(swatch)=>swatch.label>
      <div class=`flex h-20 flex-col justify-end rounded-lg p-3 text-xs font-medium ${swatch.surface}`>
        ${swatch.label}
      </div>
    </for>
  </div>

  <p>
    The swatches above are rendered with the live tokens on this page — toggle the theme in the
    header and they follow.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tokens">Theme Tokens</h2>

  <p>
    These are defined under <code>:root</code> and re-defined under <code>.dark</code>, then
    exposed to Tailwind through <code>@theme inline</code>.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Token</th>
        <th>What it controls</th>
        <th>Used by</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <for|entry| of=TOKENS by=(entry)=>entry.token>
        <tr>
          <td><code>${entry.token}</code></td>
          <td>${entry.controls}</td>
          <td>${entry.usedBy}</td>
        </tr>
      </for>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="base-colors">Base Colors</h2>

  <p>
    The registry publishes five theme items, one per shadcn base color. They differ only in the hue
    and chroma of the neutral ramp — every token name is identical, so components behave the same
    whichever you pick.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Base color</th>
        <th>Registry item</th>
        <th>Character</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <for|base| of=BASE_COLORS by=(base)=>base.name>
        <tr>
          <td><code>${base.name}</code></td>
          <td><code>${base.item}</code></td>
          <td>${base.note}</td>
        </tr>
      </for>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <code-block language="bash" code=INSTALL_BASE/>

  <p>
    Each item carries its palette twice: as the full <code>globals.css</code> file, and as
    <code>cssVars</code> in the registry JSON so the shadcn CLI can merge the variables into a
    stylesheet you already have. Whichever you install, the file lands at
    <code>src/styles/globals.css</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="radius">Radius Scale</h2>

  <p>
    <code>--radius</code> is the single source of truth for corner rounding. The scale is derived
    from it with fixed pixel offsets, so changing one value re-rounds the whole UI.
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" filename="src/styles/globals.css" code=RADIUS_SCALE/>

  <p>
    Set <code>--radius: 0</code> for square corners, or push it up to <code>1rem</code> for a
    softer look. Because the small sizes subtract a fixed amount rather than scaling
    proportionally, very small <code>--radius</code> values clamp to square rather than going
    negative.
  </p>

  <h2 id="customizing">Customizing</h2>

  <p>
    Override tokens in your own stylesheet, after the registry import. You do not need to fork
    <code>globals.css</code> to change a color.
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" filename="src/styles/globals.css" code=OVERRIDE/>

  <p>
    Remember to override the <code>.dark</code> value too. A brand color tuned for a white
    background is usually too dark on a near-black one — that is why every token in the file is
    defined twice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="adding-tokens">Adding New Tokens</h2>

  <p>
    A new token takes three steps: define it for light, define it for dark, and register it with
    Tailwind so utilities are generated.
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=ADD_TOKEN/>

  <p>
    <code>@theme inline</code> matters here. Without the <code>--color-*</code> entry, Tailwind
    has no <code>bg-warning</code> utility to emit and the class silently does nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dark">Dark Mode</h2>

  <p>
    Dark mode is the same tokens redefined under a <code>.dark</code> class. Our stylesheet
    declares the variant explicitly:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code="@custom-variant dark (&:is(.dark *));"/>

  <p>
    Everything about toggling that class — including the inline script that prevents a flash of the
    wrong theme — is covered in <a href="/docs/dark-mode">Dark Mode</a>.
  </p>
</docs-article>
